{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Liouville","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Floor.Defs"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.eq_const_mul_of_entire_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.eq_const_mul_of_entire_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L153-L162","doc":"Pointwise form of `ratio_const_of_entire_ratio`, valid **off the divisor**:\nwherever `Φ` is nonzero, `Ψ = c · Φ` with the same constant. This is how the\nrigidity conclusion is consumed in practice — the identity is asserted on the\ncomplement of the pole/zero lattice, not on all of `ℂ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.ratio_const_of_entire_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.ratio_const_of_entire_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L129-L151","doc":"**Rigidity bridge, divisor form — the shape the Faddeev application actually\nhas.** `ratio_const_of_doublyPeriodic` above requires `Φ` to be *nowhere zero*.\nFaddeev's quantum dilogarithm `Φ_b` does **not** satisfy that: it is meromorphic\nwith a genuine zero/pole lattice (poles at `c_b + i(mb + nb⁻¹)`, zeros at the\nreflected lattice), so the bridge as stated does not apply to it directly — a\nhypothesis/application mismatch worth naming rather than working around.\n\nWhat the open input `hratio` (bean `qou-cons`) actually delivers is weaker and\nbetter matched: two `FaddeevPhi` solutions **share** their divisor, so the\n*ratio* is entire even though neither factor is zero-free. This variant takes\nexactly that as its hypothesis, and is therefore the form a discharged `hratio`\nplugs into. Together with `hibinv` (bean `qou-rp5f`, supplying `hper` off the\ndual pole lattice) it yields constancy of the ratio.\n\nStrictly weaker than `ratio_const_of_doublyPeriodic`: it assumes nothing about\nthe zeros of `Φ`, and correspondingly concludes about the ratio rather than\nabout `Ψ` pointwise (see `eq_const_mul_of_entire_ratio` for the pointwise form\naway from the divisor). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.ratio_const_of_doublyPeriodic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.ratio_const_of_doublyPeriodic","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L112-L127","doc":"**Rigidity bridge — the reduced form of `PentagonRigidity.uniqueUpToScalar`.**\nIf two entire functions `Φ, Ψ` with `Φ` nowhere zero have a ratio that is genuinely\nperiodic (prefactors cancelled) under both vectors of an `ℝ`-basis of `ℂ`, then\n`Ψ = c · Φ` for a constant `c`. This is exactly the Faddeev–Volkov rigidity content\nthe L4 pentagon file (`QOU.QBeta.CyclicDilogPentagon`) carried as an open hypothesis:\nthe two remaining inputs are now explicit and *smaller* — `Ψ/Φ` is entire (the zeros\ncoincide) and the two quasi-period ratios cancel to genuine periods with independent\nperiods (`b` a basis) — while the doubly-periodic ⇒ constant Liouville step is proved\nabove. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.const_of_doublyPeriodic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.const_of_doublyPeriodic","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L69-L110","doc":"**Liouville for doubly-periodic entire functions.** A differentiable\n`f : ℂ → ℂ` periodic under both vectors of an `ℝ`-basis `b` of `ℂ` is constant.\nReducing the two `ℝ`-coordinates of `z` modulo `1` lands `z`, up to a lattice\ntranslation `f` ignores, in the closed ball of radius `‖b 0‖ + ‖b 1‖`; that ball is\ncompact (`ℂ` proper), so `range f` is bounded and Liouville forces a constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.fract_smul_add_floor_zsmul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.fract_smul_add_floor_zsmul","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L62-L67","doc":"Single-coordinate `fract`/`floor` split for a `ℝ`-scalar acting on `ℂ`:\n`Int.fract r • v + ⌊r⌋ • v = r • v` (the integer part is a `ℤ`-multiple, the\nfractional part stays in `[0,1)`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.periodic_zsmul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.periodic_zsmul","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/DoublyPeriodicLiouville.lean#L45-L60","doc":"**Periodicity under a single period propagates to its integer multiples.** If\n`f (z + c) = f z` for all `z`, then `f (z + a • c) = f z` for every `a : ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}